1
00:00:23,389 --> 00:00:28,593
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this is Sutta on the seven awakening factors.

2
00:00:28,613 --> 00:00:42,564
[SPEAKER_01]: It begins, when a bhikkhu is accomplished in virtue, composure, understanding, and who has full knowledge and free, freedom, the sight of them is very helpful.

3
00:00:42,944 --> 00:00:46,387
[SPEAKER_01]: To hear them, to approach them, to respect them, to recollect them,

4
00:00:53,629 --> 00:00:54,729
[SPEAKER_01]: And why is that?

5
00:00:55,389 --> 00:01:05,252
[SPEAKER_01]: Because after hearing the teaching of such renunciants, a renunciant will live withdrawn in both body and mind.

6
00:01:07,432 --> 00:01:20,255
[SPEAKER_01]: And then it continues, as they recollect and think about that teaching, at such a time, a renunciant has obtained or activated the awakening factor of mindfulness.

7
00:01:20,655 --> 00:01:22,336
[SPEAKER_01]: They develop it and perfect it.

8
00:01:34,847 --> 00:01:36,067
[SPEAKER_00]: You are collected.

9
00:01:36,807 --> 00:01:38,387
[SPEAKER_00]: You're established in the right context.

10
00:01:38,627 --> 00:01:41,328
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, you wouldn't be able to think about the particulars of the teaching.

11
00:01:48,629 --> 00:01:50,229
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now, if I...

12
00:01:51,569 --> 00:01:59,091
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I meet some... you meet someone who's a... who's... has... who's understood the dhamma, and then you reflect on that teaching.

13
00:02:01,060 --> 00:02:04,902
[SPEAKER_01]: I have the awakening factor of mindfulness.

14
00:02:04,962 --> 00:02:06,343
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I'm thinking about the teaching.

15
00:02:06,823 --> 00:02:12,846
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, if you are thinking about the actual teaching, in other words, if you understood them correctly, then yes.

16
00:02:14,187 --> 00:02:14,687
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like...

17
00:02:14,907 --> 00:02:19,269
[SPEAKER_00]: So, thinking about the dhamma is not an enlightenment factor.

18
00:02:21,671 --> 00:02:30,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, thinking about the information you have on dhamma, that's not the same as understanding the meaning, the core of the dhamma.

19
00:02:32,117 --> 00:02:40,041
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, if you understood the dhamma, then, when you think about it, the enlightenment factors will be brought to fulfillment.

20
00:02:41,882 --> 00:02:47,385
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you haven't understood the dhamma for yourself, you can think about the dhamma all you want.

21
00:02:47,985 --> 00:02:52,788
[SPEAKER_00]: About it, it won't be on the level of enlightenment factors, because it's not on the level of insight.

22
00:02:53,748 --> 00:02:56,970
[SPEAKER_00]: So, but before we get into discussion, what are the enlightenment factors?

23
00:02:57,891 --> 00:03:00,172
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindfulness, and then

24
00:03:01,281 --> 00:03:16,565
[SPEAKER_01]: dhamma-vicāya, investigation into dhammas, effort, joy, calm, composure, and equanimity.

25
00:03:16,665 --> 00:03:17,966
[SPEAKER_00]: Equanimity, yeah.

26
00:03:19,566 --> 00:03:20,807
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's... yeah.

27
00:03:20,827 --> 00:03:22,427
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the first thing, basically.

28
00:03:22,447 --> 00:03:28,729
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the... see... and that's kind of the problem with most of the...

29
00:03:30,860 --> 00:03:37,565
[SPEAKER_00]: Things you read in the suttas, it's that... it's been so long ago, since they were taught, they were remembered.

30
00:03:37,625 --> 00:03:45,791
[SPEAKER_00]: And the only way to remember those things was through these, like, linear lists, like 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, or 4 of this, or 8 of that, or 12 of this.

31
00:03:46,671 --> 00:03:48,993
[SPEAKER_00]: And people then be... and that's just mere information.

32
00:03:49,353 --> 00:03:51,055
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just... just pure information.

33
00:03:51,175 --> 00:03:54,417
[SPEAKER_00]: Only seven enlightenment factors to somebody who doesn't have a clue.

34
00:03:55,077 --> 00:03:58,620
[SPEAKER_00]: Somebody who is enlightened, he doesn't even need to... he doesn't...

35
00:04:04,418 --> 00:04:11,151
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's like the important thing to realize.

36
00:04:16,432 --> 00:04:17,813
[SPEAKER_00]: It's exactly the same thing.

37
00:04:17,853 --> 00:04:21,675
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, as many so to say.

38
00:04:21,715 --> 00:04:21,855
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

39
00:04:21,895 --> 00:04:23,997
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like the right view and right mindfulness.

40
00:04:24,097 --> 00:04:28,939
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, whether it's the right effort through that, it's right because of the right view and the right mindfulness.

41
00:04:28,959 --> 00:04:33,302
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether it's the right speech through that, it's right because of the right view and right mindfulness.

42
00:04:33,922 --> 00:04:42,107
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like this right view comes first, and then, mindfulness through that is right, and the direction of your effort is right, or your livelihood is right,

43
00:04:42,538 --> 00:04:45,439
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not like I develop the right view, then I go for the second step.

44
00:04:45,539 --> 00:04:48,100
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a step-by-step arrangement.

45
00:04:48,620 --> 00:04:53,082
[SPEAKER_00]: And neither is seven enlightenment factors, which is exactly... See, he goes and hears the wise people.

46
00:04:53,422 --> 00:04:55,863
[SPEAKER_00]: He understands what they teach.

47
00:04:56,203 --> 00:04:57,483
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he actually understands.

48
00:04:58,083 --> 00:05:00,044
[SPEAKER_00]: He develops... he becomes a sotāpanna.

49
00:05:00,064 --> 00:05:01,264
[SPEAKER_00]: He develops the right view.

50
00:05:02,065 --> 00:05:11,328
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, on the basis of that correct view, standpoint, when he thinks about dhamma, when he is mindful, those enlightenment factors develop.

51
00:05:12,323 --> 00:05:16,987
[SPEAKER_00]: But not by not having the right view and thinking about the list of enlightenment factors.

52
00:05:17,987 --> 00:05:24,212
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, if I do these things, these seven things, they will lead me to enlightenment.

53
00:05:24,232 --> 00:05:24,393
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

54
00:05:24,413 --> 00:05:31,338
[SPEAKER_00]: If I do step by step these seven steps, it will culminate in complete enlightenment.

55
00:05:31,438 --> 00:05:32,319
[SPEAKER_00]: But I have no clue

56
00:05:33,403 --> 00:05:38,825
[SPEAKER_00]: What that enlightenment is, I have no right view in regard to the, you know, escape from suffering and where the practice is.

57
00:05:39,406 --> 00:05:50,991
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'll just basically use the information that I have on the level of lists, and collections, and steps, and methods, and instructions, and I'll adhere to it with, like, hopefully, it will kind of bring me the result.

58
00:05:52,151 --> 00:05:56,373
[SPEAKER_00]: But, as the opening video read, it already says, no, first you need to understand.

59
00:05:57,591 --> 00:06:00,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, these factors will come to fulfillment and development.

60
00:06:00,292 --> 00:06:04,874
[SPEAKER_00]: So, without understanding, without a right view, there are no seven enlightenment factors in you.

61
00:06:05,054 --> 00:06:06,734
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter how much you think about them.

62
00:06:08,615 --> 00:06:17,058
[SPEAKER_00]: If, on the other hand, you don't think about them as this methodical, list-by-list, step-by-step instructions,

63
00:06:17,868 --> 00:06:19,748
[SPEAKER_00]: But actually, try to understand the nature.

64
00:06:20,349 --> 00:06:21,709
[SPEAKER_00]: What is correct mindfulness?

65
00:06:22,109 --> 00:06:24,649
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the correct investigations of phenomena?

66
00:06:25,250 --> 00:06:26,710
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the correct effort?

67
00:06:26,750 --> 00:06:27,890
[SPEAKER_00]: What is samādhi?

68
00:06:27,910 --> 00:06:30,651
[SPEAKER_00]: Samādhi comes just before equanimity.

69
00:06:31,531 --> 00:06:35,092
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, here I am, believing I can practice samādhi even without the right view.

70
00:06:35,512 --> 00:06:37,212
[SPEAKER_00]: There will somehow result in enlightenment.

71
00:06:37,252 --> 00:06:41,453
[SPEAKER_00]: But as an enlightenment factor is just one towards... just before, like, calling

72
00:06:43,161 --> 00:06:45,063
[SPEAKER_00]: I have not properly fulfilled samādhi.

73
00:06:45,783 --> 00:06:48,506
[SPEAKER_00]: And there are all these things that I need to develop beforehand.

74
00:06:49,546 --> 00:06:51,748
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that you can infer from reading the sutta.

75
00:06:51,768 --> 00:06:54,731
[SPEAKER_00]: So, ah, have I developed all these things beforehand?

76
00:06:54,791 --> 00:07:05,460
[SPEAKER_00]: Have I truly developed the right view that then makes me correctly mindful, that then makes me correctly thinking about the dhammas, that then brings the joy and calm and so on?

77
00:07:06,301 --> 00:07:09,183
[SPEAKER_00]: Or am I just fabricating joy or hoping for a calm

78
00:07:09,732 --> 00:07:21,936
[SPEAKER_00]: As a kind of magical result of my method, of my technique, so that I don't have to drill the work and go through the painful process of trying to gain the understanding.

79
00:07:21,956 --> 00:07:30,659
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so these factors are factors of an awakened person, that he's developed, the awakened person is developing these factors.

80
00:07:30,699 --> 00:07:39,122
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, these are factors, again, like many other things mentioned in the suttas, are the factors for somebody who has the right view.

81
00:07:39,999 --> 00:07:57,648
[SPEAKER_00]: So, somebody who knows what... how to get to the end result, how to... how to fully achieve enlightenment, but hasn't done it yet, so these factors are the factors that he should dwell on and contemplate, and... and they will come to fulfillment if he just follows the direction of his own right view.

82
00:08:00,755 --> 00:08:10,000
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the usefulness of why the Buddha listed these seven factors is for those who have kind of attained to the right view, but haven't completed the work.

83
00:08:10,660 --> 00:08:12,341
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's a good reminder to them.

84
00:08:12,682 --> 00:08:14,162
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, look, it's only these things.

85
00:08:14,242 --> 00:08:18,765
[SPEAKER_00]: These are seven aspects of the same thing that you already have, that you already understood.

86
00:08:19,298 --> 00:08:21,680
[SPEAKER_00]: That already freed you partially.

87
00:08:22,300 --> 00:08:28,985
[SPEAKER_00]: So, dwell on these seven aspects and they will in return basically bring other aspects to fulfillment.

88
00:08:29,345 --> 00:08:32,947
[SPEAKER_01]: I remember first coming across it and trying to do these things.

89
00:08:33,688 --> 00:08:40,152
[SPEAKER_01]: And you sitting there meditating, whatever, watching the breath, and then you try to look for those things.

90
00:08:40,232 --> 00:08:41,093
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm making an effort.

91
00:08:41,333 --> 00:08:41,533
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

92
00:08:41,713 --> 00:08:42,434
[SPEAKER_01]: I am mindful.

93
00:08:43,354 --> 00:08:43,975
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I?

94
00:08:43,995 --> 00:08:44,975
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I'm investigating.

95
00:08:45,136 --> 00:08:45,476
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

96
00:08:45,696 --> 00:08:46,557
[SPEAKER_01]: I feel quite calm.

97
00:08:46,717 --> 00:08:47,197
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm happy.

98
00:08:47,863 --> 00:08:50,625
[SPEAKER_01]: And, you know, I'm peaceful, so... Oh, he somehow composed a samādhi?

99
00:08:50,705 --> 00:08:52,326
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, look, although it's very close to nibbāna.

100
00:08:52,346 --> 00:08:52,746
[SPEAKER_01]: Where is it?

101
00:08:52,766 --> 00:08:53,166
[SPEAKER_00]: Where is it?

102
00:08:53,286 --> 00:08:56,448
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, just keep it going, and then... Yeah, yeah.

103
00:08:57,149 --> 00:09:12,738
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that is... that is sort of the... quite... like a normal, especially, you know, due to the whole, like, Abhidhamma stuff and commentarial, where the whole dhamma is presented as just basically a collection of lists and... and myriad of, like, information and this and... and parallels and... and so on.

104
00:09:12,778 --> 00:09:14,079
[SPEAKER_00]: But none of that is actually the d

105
00:09:29,546 --> 00:09:35,868
[SPEAKER_00]: Without that, there is no dhamma, even if you know all of the suttas by heart, and all of the information you've collected.

106
00:09:38,789 --> 00:09:54,554
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's something that people don't necessarily know, because, again, you know, you're presented with so much stuff today, and so many different texts, so then you come to... you come and read various things, and then you immediately, naturally, sort of, try to make sense out of it.

107
00:09:54,996 --> 00:09:57,317
[SPEAKER_00]: But you... you're not aware that you're actually reading into it.

108
00:09:57,437 --> 00:10:00,879
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not... you're not... you're not transparent enough.

109
00:10:00,959 --> 00:10:02,260
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have the right basis.

110
00:10:02,620 --> 00:10:04,461
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have the gradual training developed.

111
00:10:04,982 --> 00:10:11,205
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you come across this information, you're kind of taking it as information that's being misunderstood for understanding.

112
00:10:11,826 --> 00:10:14,567
[SPEAKER_00]: And most people's understanding is that, just a collection of information.

113
00:10:15,268 --> 00:10:15,928
[SPEAKER_00]: And when they sort of

114
00:10:16,669 --> 00:10:31,142
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a form of understanding, but it's not a form of understanding that pertains on that individual, personal level, internally, to be known by the wise, as the Buddha says.

115
00:10:31,903 --> 00:10:40,871
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, the one that pertains to freedom from suffering, for which no amount of information will suffice to free you.

116
00:10:41,031 --> 00:10:43,573
[SPEAKER_00]: Only that insight into Four Noble Truths would.

117
00:10:48,192 --> 00:10:50,854
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the important thing to realize.

118
00:10:51,434 --> 00:10:51,694
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

119
00:10:51,734 --> 00:11:11,488
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you don't have the right view, that must be your your sole kind of goal, is to develop right view, insert into freedom from suffering, to know for yourself the origin, the suffering, the origin, the cessation, and the path that leads to cessation of suffering.

120
00:11:14,092 --> 00:11:18,795
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when that's understood, then, within that understanding, you have the right mindfulness.

121
00:11:18,855 --> 00:11:20,156
[SPEAKER_00]: You have the Noble Eightfold Path.

122
00:11:20,176 --> 00:11:21,696
[SPEAKER_00]: You have the Seven Enlightenment Factors.

123
00:11:22,097 --> 00:11:23,477
[SPEAKER_00]: You have the Four Right Strivings.

124
00:11:23,517 --> 00:11:25,539
[SPEAKER_00]: Whichever aspect you put, it's within that.

125
00:11:26,899 --> 00:11:28,440
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will know that.

126
00:11:28,460 --> 00:11:36,245
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, through developing this one insight and state of mind that I have, that I kind of work for, I am developing this.

127
00:11:36,405 --> 00:11:37,906
[SPEAKER_00]: And now, if you want to develop it more,

128
00:11:39,172 --> 00:11:51,477
[SPEAKER_00]: Astutely, more precisely, more efficiently, you then study further the information about the seven enlightenment factors, so that you can apply it correctly within that basis that you have.

129
00:11:52,018 --> 00:11:55,259
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you don't have the basis, it's just a collection of information.

130
00:11:56,460 --> 00:11:58,280
[SPEAKER_00]: And dangerous in a way.

131
00:11:58,300 --> 00:12:06,144
[SPEAKER_00]: Very dangerous, because information is just too easy to be misconceived, confused, mistaken for understanding.

132
00:12:06,914 --> 00:12:12,635
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, when you have so much information or, like, the... Again, I don't want to sound out too much, like, too much thinking or something.

133
00:12:14,615 --> 00:12:16,076
[SPEAKER_00]: Or too much information is bad.

134
00:12:17,636 --> 00:12:20,196
[SPEAKER_00]: It's dangerous because of the wrong attitude underneath.

135
00:12:20,736 --> 00:12:26,337
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, whether you have the information or not, the wrong attitude is really what keeps you ignorant.

136
00:12:29,078 --> 00:12:32,578
[SPEAKER_00]: So, don't think now, like, that if you are getting...

137
00:12:32,818 --> 00:12:35,739
[SPEAKER_00]: I must not get all this information because it's dangerous.

138
00:12:36,737 --> 00:12:41,418
[SPEAKER_00]: Your ignorance is dangerous, so you have to always keep perspective in regard to that.

139
00:12:41,798 --> 00:12:54,161
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I am ignorant and I'm collecting information now, I'm learning about the suttas, I'm not taking anything for granted, I'm taking everything with a pinch of salt, until I know for myself the way out of suffering.

140
00:12:54,221 --> 00:12:55,581
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the only criteria.

141
00:12:55,661 --> 00:12:59,122
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing shorter than sotāpatti should be a criteria of success.

142
00:13:00,602 --> 00:13:02,903
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you don't have that authentic attitude,

143
00:13:03,344 --> 00:13:23,799
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, whether you have a little bit of information or a lot of information, well, it makes no difference, because you're already obscured by yourself, by being ignorant, by not having that right attitude, that pertains to freedom from suffering, as the only measure of understanding, not the satisfaction of, you know, making the sense out of information and so on.

144
00:13:34,817 --> 00:13:40,320
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll just continue as the first one.

145
00:13:40,360 --> 00:13:51,386
[SPEAKER_01]: As they recollect and think about that teaching, at such a time, a renunciant, with the right view, has obtained the awakening factor of mindfulness.

146
00:13:52,506 --> 00:13:53,927
[SPEAKER_01]: They develop it and perfect it.

147
00:13:55,868 --> 00:14:03,192
[SPEAKER_01]: And when they investigate and explore and inquire into their teaching, wisdom, at such a time, they have that awakening factor of

148
00:14:04,287 --> 00:14:04,927
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

149
00:14:05,307 --> 00:14:13,730
[SPEAKER_00]: So, see, another aspect of that is that all the enlightenment factors are kind of peripheral, secondary.

150
00:14:15,211 --> 00:14:18,732
[SPEAKER_00]: When he investigates, the mindfulness gets developed.

151
00:14:19,392 --> 00:14:24,314
[SPEAKER_00]: When he thinks and ponders about that teaching he understood, dhammavīcāya, it gets developed.

152
00:14:24,994 --> 00:14:28,835
[SPEAKER_00]: Not, he thinks about dhammavīcāya and that's how dhammavīcāya gets developed.

153
00:14:30,512 --> 00:14:34,633
[SPEAKER_00]: He thinks about the dhamma, ponders on dhamma, or fortifies his understanding of dhamma.

154
00:14:34,673 --> 00:14:38,193
[SPEAKER_00]: That effort is the development of dhammavīcchā, secondarily.

155
00:14:42,114 --> 00:14:43,794
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't think about dhammavīcchā.

156
00:14:43,954 --> 00:14:48,435
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you know, I think about dhammavīcchā now, and that's dhammavīcchā, and I see dhammavīcchā being developed.

157
00:14:48,835 --> 00:14:49,715
[SPEAKER_00]: No, he clearly says that.

158
00:14:49,735 --> 00:14:51,776
[SPEAKER_00]: When he does this, mindfulness gets developed.

159
00:14:51,916 --> 00:14:54,516
[SPEAKER_00]: While he's doing this, dhammavīcchā has developed.

160
00:14:56,196 --> 00:14:59,037
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so, as he investigates

161
00:15:05,075 --> 00:15:08,376
[SPEAKER_01]: Indirectly, again.

162
00:15:08,396 --> 00:15:11,578
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, striving.

163
00:15:12,238 --> 00:15:14,399
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

164
00:15:14,759 --> 00:15:29,005
[SPEAKER_00]: While he's making the effort to clarify what he understood, to fortify, to sustain, to protect that context, everything we talk about, all that, that he invests in that, brings the the enlightenment factor of effort to fulfillment.

165
00:15:30,285 --> 00:15:34,147
[SPEAKER_00]: Not, oh, I shall fulfill this effort towards enlightenment.

166
00:15:39,674 --> 00:15:40,234
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the point.

167
00:15:40,334 --> 00:15:41,195
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he's striving.

168
00:15:41,495 --> 00:15:46,259
[SPEAKER_00]: He's striving towards the goal of freedom from suffering that he understood but hasn't fully fortified.

169
00:15:48,160 --> 00:16:00,128
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, the direction of the right view, the understanding that you get through the right view, the effort you're putting into furthering that understanding is the enlightenment factor of effort and striving.

170
00:16:08,280 --> 00:16:11,981
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and then he's attained that enlightenment factor of joy.

171
00:16:12,881 --> 00:16:13,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

172
00:16:13,502 --> 00:16:20,503
[SPEAKER_00]: So, joy arises on its own, as a result, as a product of his... As a by-product, yeah.

173
00:16:20,703 --> 00:16:21,404
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

174
00:16:21,484 --> 00:16:26,945
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like, oh, I shall be joyful and now let me bring this joy now, because that's another factor that the Buddha talks about.

175
00:16:27,525 --> 00:16:27,685
No.

176
00:16:28,105 --> 00:16:29,566
[SPEAKER_00]: Follow the direction of the right view.

177
00:16:30,566 --> 00:16:31,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't fall from it.

178
00:16:31,686 --> 00:16:32,406
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't neglect it.

179
00:16:32,626 --> 00:16:34,267
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't become negligent or complacent.

180
00:16:34,926 --> 00:16:38,147
[SPEAKER_00]: Joy will arise on account of the effort that you invested in following that direction.

181
00:16:40,708 --> 00:16:44,629
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when that mind is joyous, the body and mind become calm.

182
00:16:45,009 --> 00:16:45,169
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

183
00:16:45,449 --> 00:16:46,549
[SPEAKER_00]: Unified and calm.

184
00:16:47,390 --> 00:16:49,410
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, that's not something he does.

185
00:16:49,990 --> 00:16:52,531
[SPEAKER_00]: Let me... let me unify this now.

186
00:16:54,552 --> 00:16:56,472
[SPEAKER_00]: Let me fulfill that which understood.

187
00:16:56,592 --> 00:16:59,673
[SPEAKER_00]: Let me dwell further on the understanding of the Four Noble Truths.

188
00:16:59,753 --> 00:17:00,714
[SPEAKER_00]: Let me fortify them.

189
00:17:00,834 --> 00:17:02,154
[SPEAKER_00]: And that will calm down your mind.

190
00:17:02,648 --> 00:17:05,550
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will establish samādhi like that.

191
00:17:05,610 --> 00:17:06,910
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the establishment of samādhi.

192
00:17:10,572 --> 00:17:17,016
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not the, oh, let me directly attend to this that will concentrate my mind on account of my effort to concentrate my mind.

193
00:17:17,956 --> 00:17:20,758
[SPEAKER_00]: It's completely... You say, go in the direction of that right view.

194
00:17:20,818 --> 00:17:27,861
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, go in the direction of the right understanding, the right view, which is the right mindfulness, the correct investigation of phenomena, and so on.

195
00:17:29,102 --> 00:17:30,983
[SPEAKER_00]: And that results in composure.

196
00:17:31,985 --> 00:17:37,828
[SPEAKER_00]: towards that direction, being fully committed, directed, composed, uh... in... in that one way.

197
00:17:39,228 --> 00:17:40,949
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... that's one-pointedness.

198
00:17:41,029 --> 00:17:43,471
[SPEAKER_00]: Not one-pointedness of your tip of your nose or something.

199
00:17:44,891 --> 00:17:49,794
[SPEAKER_00]: It's one-pointedness of the direction towards freedom from suffering, composed in that one direction.

200
00:17:50,574 --> 00:17:54,696
[SPEAKER_00]: That's like one way only, for the one who practices rightly the satipaṭṭhānas.

201
00:17:55,636 --> 00:17:56,517
[SPEAKER_00]: So, no right view

202
00:17:57,408 --> 00:18:06,154
[SPEAKER_00]: No enlightenment factors, no right view, no samādhi, no right view, no freedom from suffering, no understanding, no wisdom, no equanimity.

203
00:18:07,936 --> 00:18:19,524
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you think you have samādhi, and you think you have equanimity, and you think you have joy, without a right view, knowing that you don't have the right view, take them with a pinch of salt.

204
00:18:20,404 --> 00:18:23,967
[SPEAKER_00]: Recognize that they might not be the one, the correct.

205
00:18:24,374 --> 00:18:27,395
[SPEAKER_00]: Samādhi and correct equanimity, because there is no right view.

206
00:18:28,515 --> 00:18:30,636
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not to say, oh, there is no samādhi at all.

207
00:18:30,656 --> 00:18:32,137
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no form of samādhi at all.

208
00:18:32,217 --> 00:18:38,779
[SPEAKER_00]: But, it's important to recognize that the wrong samādhi, mistaken to be the right samādhi, cannot magically become the right samādhi.

209
00:18:39,359 --> 00:18:50,843
[SPEAKER_00]: Only fully abandoning that with the samādhi of the wrong kind that you have, the equanimity and peace of the wrong kind that you have, fully abandoning it, in order to get the right view,

210
00:18:51,283 --> 00:18:57,386
[SPEAKER_00]: Through exposure of that recognition to, you know, towards suffering and so on, that's how you can get, then, the right samādhi.

211
00:18:58,547 --> 00:19:07,532
[SPEAKER_00]: Because sometimes people might think, oh, well, you know, I know my samādhi is not right, but if I keep investing any effort in this wrong samādhi, it will become right.

212
00:19:07,892 --> 00:19:08,052
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

213
00:19:09,593 --> 00:19:14,776
[SPEAKER_00]: Right samādhi is the result of right view and correctly directed mind.

214
00:19:15,716 --> 00:19:20,179
[SPEAKER_00]: Right samādhi is not the result of repairing the wrong samādhi.

215
00:19:21,102 --> 00:19:22,302
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already the wrong view.

216
00:19:22,382 --> 00:19:27,144
[SPEAKER_00]: Thinking you can directly attend to your samādhi as a form of concentration that will then result in some liberation.

217
00:19:27,964 --> 00:19:34,946
[SPEAKER_00]: See, you don't concentrate your mind there towards your own concentration and develop your samādhi through focusing and so on.

218
00:19:35,666 --> 00:19:46,890
[SPEAKER_00]: You develop samādhi by following the right view, which is sort of guided by the right mindfulness and correct investigation of the factors, and joy, and calm, and effort.

219
00:19:48,270 --> 00:19:50,231
[SPEAKER_00]: And that becomes a unification of the mind.

220
00:19:51,250 --> 00:19:53,371
[SPEAKER_00]: It's equally secondary, the right smādhi.

221
00:19:53,412 --> 00:19:54,132
[SPEAKER_00]: It's indirect.

222
00:19:54,712 --> 00:19:59,816
[SPEAKER_00]: It's... it's basically peripheral from where you are making your effort towards understanding freedom from suffering.

223
00:20:05,460 --> 00:20:16,387
[SPEAKER_01]: It's... it's... this whole section is found in Majjhima Nikaya 117.

224
00:20:18,268 --> 00:20:20,590
[SPEAKER_01]: These three states run and circle around

225
00:20:21,217 --> 00:20:26,660
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's always those three.

226
00:20:26,861 --> 00:20:42,030
[SPEAKER_00]: If you have the right view, means there is a degree of right effort.

227
00:20:42,710 --> 00:20:45,332
[SPEAKER_00]: And there certainly means there is a degree of right mindfulness.

228
00:20:45,352 --> 00:20:50,175
[SPEAKER_00]: If you have right mindfulness, the right view is there, and right effort is there.

229
00:20:51,076 --> 00:20:54,840
[SPEAKER_00]: If you have right effort, the right view is brought back, and the right mindfulness is there.

230
00:20:56,841 --> 00:20:57,382
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

231
00:20:57,402 --> 00:20:58,223
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not linear.

232
00:20:58,323 --> 00:21:02,867
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you try to understand them on the level of their linear information, you will fail.

233
00:21:03,308 --> 00:21:07,912
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they cannot be attended to directly, and be understood in their nature.

234
00:21:08,412 --> 00:21:10,715
[SPEAKER_00]: You can attend information directly.

235
00:21:11,195 --> 00:21:13,157
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, there's one, two, three, four, five, six, seven.

236
00:21:14,535 --> 00:21:16,496
[SPEAKER_00]: But what does that information pertain to?

237
00:21:16,637 --> 00:21:21,160
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, pertain to... pertains to understanding freedom from suffering.

238
00:21:21,420 --> 00:21:21,981
[SPEAKER_00]: What suffering?

239
00:21:22,001 --> 00:21:24,022
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, suffering that I'm liable here and now.

240
00:21:24,703 --> 00:21:42,816
[SPEAKER_00]: So, instead of me making the effort to make... to sort of draw connections and parallels that nobody else thought of, and somehow get a magical insight on account of it, between these seven factors, let me dwell... dwell on the level of my liability to suffering, endure it,

241
00:21:43,648 --> 00:21:55,390
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't try to get rid of it or manage it, which is why you always try to jump and pounce on the information, hoping it will bring you freedom, because you want to get rid of the liability that is painful.

242
00:21:57,110 --> 00:22:10,793
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the efforts to understand the dhamma, if they're directed towards basically manipulating and sort of drawing parallels between information that you collected about a dhamma, it's just going to become another form of management.

243
00:22:12,903 --> 00:22:16,964
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, you need information.

244
00:22:17,004 --> 00:22:18,304
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to be told what to do.

245
00:22:18,984 --> 00:22:30,947
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you refuse to allow yourself, basically, to come back onto the level that it matters, where that applies, you're just gonna stay on the level of playing with information for the rest of your life.

246
00:22:31,827 --> 00:22:37,808
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you refuse to give up the attitude of trying to manage your liability to suffering by understanding information.

247
00:22:39,173 --> 00:22:45,195
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's psychotherapy, that's psychology, that's any kind of worldly way of dealing with suffering and pain.

248
00:22:45,936 --> 00:22:47,396
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all on the level of management.

249
00:22:48,757 --> 00:22:50,558
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's only relatively efficient.

250
00:22:52,899 --> 00:23:02,522
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you learn the seven factors, you learn the Four Noble Truths, you learn the gradual training, you have more information than you need.

251
00:23:03,063 --> 00:23:08,505
[SPEAKER_00]: As we said before, back in the Buddha's day, there were no sutta collections.

252
00:23:10,735 --> 00:23:19,003
[SPEAKER_00]: All the information we have now, on account of the, you know, the nikāyas, like, monks did not have access to that.

253
00:23:19,664 --> 00:23:21,566
[SPEAKER_00]: Only Ānanda remembered all of it.

254
00:23:22,186 --> 00:23:23,127
[SPEAKER_00]: Most of the monks didn't.

255
00:23:23,668 --> 00:23:33,357
[SPEAKER_00]: And probably, vast majority of them knew maybe one story, with one instruction, and that was enough for enlightenment.

256
00:23:38,108 --> 00:23:47,351
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you know the Four Noble Truths, you know the gradual training, most importantly, you know what comes first, virtue, sensory strength, guiding of the sense doors, you have all the information you need.

257
00:23:48,871 --> 00:23:51,692
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you have to start doing that which that information tells you.

258
00:23:52,292 --> 00:23:53,332
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the catch.

259
00:23:53,952 --> 00:23:54,693
[SPEAKER_00]: People don't do it.

260
00:23:55,753 --> 00:24:04,095
[SPEAKER_00]: Even when they take on the virtue, take on the gradual training, they kind of still keep it on the level of performing the instruction gain on the level of information.

261
00:24:05,135 --> 00:24:06,956
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they don't want to endure

262
00:24:08,674 --> 00:24:18,377
[SPEAKER_00]: The dukkha, on that right level, that liability to suffering, they don't want to feel it without immediately trying to manage it, because it's too unpleasant.

263
00:24:18,437 --> 00:24:30,061
[SPEAKER_00]: If you just give up the management and just stay under that pressure of that unresolved liability to suffering on the most, on the deepest existential level.

264
00:24:32,082 --> 00:24:33,222
[SPEAKER_00]: But you have to, basically.

265
00:24:34,242 --> 00:24:36,343
[SPEAKER_00]: If the right view were to sort of

266
00:24:37,933 --> 00:24:39,474
[SPEAKER_00]: Take the root and start to grow.

267
00:24:39,934 --> 00:24:41,395
[SPEAKER_00]: It can only do on that basis.

268
00:24:41,415 --> 00:24:42,176
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what Buddha said.

269
00:24:43,397 --> 00:24:45,498
[SPEAKER_00]: If somebody... well, he teaches only two things.

270
00:24:45,518 --> 00:24:47,299
[SPEAKER_00]: Suffering and freedom from it.

271
00:24:48,420 --> 00:24:52,803
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if somebody correctly understands the nature of dukkha, there are only two outcomes of that.

272
00:24:53,683 --> 00:24:55,925
[SPEAKER_00]: Either mental derangement or noble search.

273
00:24:56,325 --> 00:25:06,051
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, okay, this, if they don't lose their mind on account of the recognition of that correct pain of liability to suffering, that existential dread,

274
00:25:06,708 --> 00:25:16,254
[SPEAKER_00]: If they don't lose the mind on account of it, then they will have no other choice but to try and correctly uproot it and seek the correct information.

275
00:25:16,754 --> 00:25:21,837
[SPEAKER_00]: They will then, sort of, you know, be the basis for them making the right effort and enduring things on the right level.

276
00:25:22,718 --> 00:25:24,799
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it comes down to that, enduring things on the right level.

277
00:25:25,079 --> 00:25:31,443
[SPEAKER_00]: If you endure things on the level of that existential dukkha without trying to manage it, get rid of it, distract yourself from it,

278
00:25:33,893 --> 00:25:39,875
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, the whole basis for the right view and everything else that comes from it is being developed.

279
00:25:39,895 --> 00:25:46,498
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's that fundamental difference between information and understanding of it.

280
00:25:47,158 --> 00:25:48,379
[SPEAKER_00]: It's two opposite directions.

281
00:25:49,599 --> 00:25:59,843
[SPEAKER_00]: So, information gives you something that needs to be understood, but only your effort to understand can bring that understanding, not more information.

282
00:25:59,903 --> 00:26:02,324
[SPEAKER_00]: More information cannot accidentally result in understanding.

283
00:26:03,276 --> 00:26:04,437
[SPEAKER_00]: Only your effort to understand.

284
00:26:08,679 --> 00:26:12,902
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, most of the monks didn't have 99% of the suttas that we have today.

285
00:26:15,884 --> 00:26:17,745
[SPEAKER_00]: And in a way, see, that was good.

286
00:26:18,065 --> 00:26:20,667
[SPEAKER_00]: Their minds were less cluttered with so many information.

287
00:26:20,767 --> 00:26:29,872
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, with that natural tendency to draw parallels between information, instead of understanding each and everyone individually on that personal basis.

288
00:26:30,933 --> 00:26:32,954
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like when a person reads about nāmarūpa,

289
00:26:33,645 --> 00:26:35,165
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, reads about the five aggregates.

290
00:26:36,225 --> 00:26:39,206
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, tries to understand how nāmarūpa relates to five aggregates.

291
00:26:40,106 --> 00:26:47,407
[SPEAKER_00]: And, drawing parallels between, develops the belief that, oh, see, I understood this now, because, look, I found the common ground, the connection.

292
00:26:48,287 --> 00:26:50,968
[SPEAKER_00]: And, that's just a connection of further information.

293
00:26:52,448 --> 00:27:03,350
[SPEAKER_00]: If you were truly authentic, the way I'm talking about, if you endured the suffering on the right level, nāmarūpa, or the five aggregates, or the six sense bases, doesn't matter which one,

294
00:27:06,929 --> 00:27:16,293
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to understand nāmarūpa, to the extent that frees you from suffering, or at least offers a direction of freedom from suffering.

295
00:27:16,833 --> 00:27:22,076
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you need to understand the five aggregates, to the extent that frees you from suffering.

296
00:27:22,196 --> 00:27:25,397
[SPEAKER_00]: Or saḷāyatana, to the extent that frees you from suffering.

297
00:27:26,368 --> 00:27:41,702
[SPEAKER_00]: Not, hmm, nāmarūpa relates to the five habits, which is all on the basis of... All of that came much later, when people have all this information, and fell away from that personal basis of recognition of their own liability to suffer.

298
00:27:42,442 --> 00:27:47,927
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, it's just too easy to start conflating information and connection between information with, like, greater understanding.

299
00:27:49,409 --> 00:27:50,730
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, got even worse.

300
00:27:50,810 --> 00:27:52,852
[SPEAKER_00]: People started studying Pali scholastically,

301
00:27:54,138 --> 00:28:00,460
[SPEAKER_00]: And in learning the etymology of the informational term, develop the belief that you understood it.

302
00:28:01,541 --> 00:28:06,062
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you truly understood viññāṇa, for example, you'll be an arahant.

303
00:28:06,442 --> 00:28:10,303
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the Buddha said, complete understanding of viññāṇa means arahantship.

304
00:28:12,984 --> 00:28:23,688
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all of that, it doesn't matter how much information you have, and how much... how dependent you became on that information, all of that can be cleaned away.

305
00:28:24,560 --> 00:28:40,573
[SPEAKER_00]: In one sweep of just re-establishing yourself, if you're brave enough and strong enough to endure it, on the level of, I am still liable to sickness, aging, death, anxiety, dread, suffering, I know no escape from it internally.

306
00:28:41,473 --> 00:28:49,600
[SPEAKER_00]: I am not able to not move when these things present themselves, which is why I always try to avoid those things from presenting themselves.

307
00:28:50,280 --> 00:28:52,382
[SPEAKER_00]: I always try to cover up when they're present.

308
00:28:53,100 --> 00:28:58,323
[SPEAKER_00]: With the possibility of their presence, I always try to ignore and distract myself from it.

309
00:29:00,044 --> 00:29:10,031
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you abandon these attitudes, and endure these things on the right level, even the little information you collected, will start slotting itself in the right place.

310
00:29:11,492 --> 00:29:13,033
[SPEAKER_00]: Because now, you got the basis.

311
00:29:13,453 --> 00:29:20,998
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the two factors for the right view, it's, yes, correct information, utterance of another, and yoniso manasikāra.

312
00:29:25,730 --> 00:29:27,094
[SPEAKER_00]: Without it, there is no right view.

313
00:29:28,960 --> 00:29:31,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you listen to the Buddha himself giving you information.

